# Deployment Guide

## {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Production Deployment

This guide covers deploying your application to an AWS EC2 instance using Docker Compose.

## Prerequisites

- An AWS account
- A domain name pointing to your server's IP address (or Elastic IP)
- SSH access to your EC2 instance
- Basic familiarity with Linux command line

## AWS EC2 Instance Setup

### 1. Launch an EC2 Instance

1. Log in to the AWS Management Console
2. Navigate to **EC2 > Launch Instance**
3. Configure the instance:
   - **Name**: {{PROJECT_NAME}}
   - **AMI**: Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS (HVM, SSD Volume Type)
   - **Instance type**: t3.small (2 vCPU, 2GB RAM minimum recommended)
   - **Key pair**: Create or select an existing SSH key pair
   - **Network settings**: Create a new security group (see Step 2)
   - **Storage**: 20GB gp3 (minimum)
4. Click **Launch Instance**

### 2. Configure Security Group

Create or edit the security group with the following inbound rules:

| Type  | Protocol | Port Range | Source    | Description          |
|-------|----------|------------|-----------|----------------------|
| SSH   | TCP      | 22         | Your IP   | SSH access           |
| HTTP  | TCP      | 80         | 0.0.0.0/0 | HTTP traffic        |
| HTTPS | TCP      | 443        | 0.0.0.0/0 | HTTPS traffic       |

> **Tip**: Restrict SSH access to your IP address or a known CIDR block rather than 0.0.0.0/0.

### 3. Allocate an Elastic IP (Recommended)

1. Navigate to **EC2 > Elastic IPs**
2. Click **Allocate Elastic IP address**
3. Select the Elastic IP, click **Actions > Associate Elastic IP address**
4. Select your EC2 instance and associate

This ensures your server keeps the same public IP after restarts.

### 4. Initial Server Setup

SSH into your instance:

```bash
ssh -i /path/to/your-key.pem ubuntu@your_server_ip
```

Create a deploy user:

```bash
# Create user
sudo adduser deploy
sudo usermod -aG sudo deploy

# Set up SSH for deploy user
sudo mkdir -p /home/deploy/.ssh
sudo cp ~/.ssh/authorized_keys /home/deploy/.ssh/
sudo chown -R deploy:deploy /home/deploy/.ssh
sudo chmod 700 /home/deploy/.ssh
sudo chmod 600 /home/deploy/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

### 5. Install Docker

```bash
# Update system
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install Docker
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh
sudo sh get-docker.sh

# Add deploy user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker deploy

# Install Docker Compose
sudo apt install docker-compose-plugin -y

# Verify installation
docker --version
docker compose version
```

### 6. Configure Firewall (UFW)

```bash
# Enable UFW
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw enable
```

### 7. Set Up Application Directory

```bash
# Switch to deploy user
su - deploy

# Create application directory
mkdir -p ~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}
cd ~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}
```

## Deploy Application

### 1. Transfer Files

From your local machine:

```bash
# Option A: Using Git (recommended)
# On server:
git clone your-repo-url ~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}

# Option B: Using rsync
rsync -avz --exclude 'node_modules' --exclude '.next' \
  ./ deploy@your_server_ip:~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}/
```

### 2. Create Production Environment File

```bash
cd ~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}
cp .env.example .env.production
nano .env.production
```

Update with production values:

```env
# Database
DB_USER=your_db_user
DB_PASSWORD=your_secure_password
DB_NAME={{PROJECT_NAME}}_prod
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://${DB_USER}:${DB_PASSWORD}@db:5432/${DB_NAME}

# App
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://yourdomain.com
NODE_ENV=production

# Add other secrets as needed
```

### 3. Set Up Nginx Configuration

Create `docker/nginx/conf.d/app.conf`:

```nginx
upstream nextjs {
    server app:3000;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name yourdomain.com www.yourdomain.com;

    location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ {
        root /var/www/certbot;
    }

    location / {
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name yourdomain.com www.yourdomain.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.com/privkey.pem;

    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256;
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;

    # Security headers
    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
    add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://nextjs;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;
    }

    # Static files caching
    location /_next/static {
        proxy_pass http://nextjs;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
    }
}
```

### 4. Obtain SSL Certificate

First, start nginx with HTTP only:

```bash
# Create initial nginx config for HTTP only
# Then run certbot to obtain certificate

docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm certbot \
  certonly --webroot --webroot-path=/var/www/certbot \
  --email your@email.com --agree-tos --no-eff-email \
  -d yourdomain.com -d www.yourdomain.com
```

### 5. Start Application

```bash
# Build and start containers
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build

# Check logs
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs -f

# Verify containers are running
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml ps
```

## Maintenance

### View Logs

```bash
# All services
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs -f

# Specific service
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs -f app
```

### Update Application

```bash
cd ~/apps/{{PROJECT_NAME}}

# Pull latest code
git pull origin main

# Rebuild and restart
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build
```

### Database Backup

```bash
# Create backup
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec db \
  pg_dump -U $DB_USER $DB_NAME > backups/backup_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).sql

# Restore backup
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec -T db \
  psql -U $DB_USER $DB_NAME < backups/your_backup.sql
```

### SSL Certificate Renewal

Certbot container automatically renews certificates. To manually renew:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm certbot renew
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec nginx nginx -s reload
```

## AWS-Specific Operations

### Instance Management

```bash
# Stop instance (from AWS CLI)
aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids i-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Start instance
aws ec2 start-instances --instance-ids i-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Reboot instance
aws ec2 reboot-instances --instance-ids i-xxxxxxxxxxxx
```

### Monitoring with CloudWatch

1. Navigate to **CloudWatch > Metrics > EC2**
2. Enable detailed monitoring for CPU, network, and disk metrics
3. Create alarms for:
   - CPU utilization > 80%
   - Status check failures
   - Disk space usage

### Setting Up CloudWatch Agent (Optional)

```bash
# Install CloudWatch agent
sudo apt install amazon-cloudwatch-agent -y

# Configure (use wizard)
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard
```

### Create AMI Backup (Snapshot)

1. Navigate to **EC2 > Instances**
2. Select your instance
3. Click **Actions > Image and templates > Create image**
4. Name it `{{PROJECT_NAME}}-backup-YYYYMMDD`

## Monitoring

### Basic Health Check

```bash
curl -f http://localhost:3000/api/health || echo "App is down!"
```

### Set Up Monitoring (Optional)

Consider setting up:
- **Uptime monitoring**: UptimeRobot, Better Uptime
- **Error tracking**: Sentry
- **Log aggregation**: CloudWatch Logs, Papertrail, Logtail
- **Server metrics**: CloudWatch, Netdata

## Troubleshooting

### Container won't start

```bash
# Check logs
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs app

# Check container status
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml ps

# Rebuild from scratch
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml down
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build --force-recreate
```

### Database connection issues

```bash
# Check database is running
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec db pg_isready

# Check connection from app container
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec app nc -zv db 5432
```

### Nginx SSL issues

```bash
# Test nginx config
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec nginx nginx -t

# Reload nginx
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec nginx nginx -s reload
```

### EC2 Instance Won't Connect

```bash
# Check security group allows SSH from your IP
aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Check instance status
aws ec2 describe-instance-status --instance-ids i-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# If locked out, use EC2 Instance Connect from the AWS Console
```

## Security Checklist

- [ ] SSH key authentication only (disable password auth)
- [ ] Security group restricts SSH to known IPs
- [ ] Firewall configured (UFW)
- [ ] SSL certificate installed
- [ ] Environment variables secured
- [ ] Database not exposed to internet
- [ ] Regular backups configured (AMI snapshots + DB dumps)
- [ ] Security updates enabled (unattended-upgrades)
- [ ] Strong database passwords
- [ ] Application secrets rotated regularly
- [ ] IAM role attached with least-privilege permissions (if using AWS services)
- [ ] Elastic IP associated (prevents IP change on restart)
